title The network the record closed -- Pond's enclosure policy declared a wall the enclosure never had
prompt Open as HUSH under the INNER LOOP Standing block -- roster cold then hot, reds first as standfast, QA on touch, the council rota, prose witnesses on prose sends, stamp-and-name, ASCII-first, READ SCOPE -- then pick up the running thread and take the Pond lane under the accrete-only gate boundary.
think ListAgents named no peer, and the process table named two. A standing_equipment_run.sh --hot from 19:32 was alive when I opened at 19:48, and four commits landed 19:36 to 19:43 -- a Sound session that closed before my first write. Dream runs beside me at /home/keeper/grain-dream, its own clone, checked by readlink /proc/<pid>/cwd rather than assumed. So one hand on this checkout, mine, and I name the other two here because REDS %291 asks for the naming rather than for a clean answer.
think The cold roster read 80 guards, 76 green, 4 red, tree_moved=no. All four are one root: construction/ITINERARY.md pinned Git nib 138cabf1b0, which git cat-file calls "not a valid object name" -- the peer's rebase rewrote the commit the card named. nib_honesty reads gone=1 verdict=FLOATING_CLAIM, and readme_metrics and geode_libraries had drifted beside it. Standfast: those closed before I opened new work.
think The lane's door on the card is Pond orbit two, whose first sentence is that each grant derives from the declaration the witness reads -- one source of truth. So the crux is not the derivation; it is whether the record the derivation would read is true. pond/enclosure_policy.kyri says of itself that it READS tools/ag/agent-jail.sh and says what it builds.
think It does not. The record declared `network off`. I read the running jail's own bwrap argument list while holding still for the roster: --unshare-pid --unshare-uts --unshare-ipc, no --unshare-net, and /etc/resolv.conf bound in. Then the direct measurement -- readlink /proc/<pid>/ns/net for a live jailed agent and for the host shell beside it both read net:[4026531833], while their PID namespaces read pid:[4026532749] against pid:[4026531836], so the instrument can tell a shared namespace from an unshared one rather than reading the same string twice. Then ai-jail --help, which offers --tailscale, --ssh, and --pictures and no network option at all. Three readings, one answer: the enclosure carries full host network and no flag the launcher could pass would close it.
think The repair is `network on`, and its consequence is the interesting part. verdict_for_network in pond/enclosure_policy.rye:288 places on `off` and HOLDS on `on`, its comment saying an open network is check-in territory and should be said out loud. So the live policy now reads place=11 hold=1 refuse=0, verdict=hold, exit 1 -- and that is the module's design working rather than a regression. The scan's ROUNDTRIP leg, which asserted verdict=place, becomes two expectations naming the holding declaration and the exact counts, so a drift in any OTHER line fails there. Naming beats counting: a leg that counted holds would pass while a different line held.
think The guard is the loom the second firing earns. tools/fixtures/p/pond_policy_launcher_scan.sh reads the launcher as answerer and the record as asker, extracting AIJAIL_FLAGS's own default and every --map and --rw-map with awk, resolving ${CLAUDE_STATE} and its siblings from the VAR="${VAR:-$LOOPS/room}" lines the script itself spells, and taking the placeholder root from the record's own persist line so nothing in the scan spells a host path.
think The closure vocabulary is the asymmetry the check rests on. In this launcher's own words a facility is closed by a flag -- --no-gpu closes the GPU, --private-home closes the home -- so a record line claiming closure must point at a flag that closes it, while a line claiming open needs no flag, because open is what an enclosure does when nobody says otherwise. gpu no is a promise; gpu yes is a description.
obs A lantern that fires twice becomes a loom, and this one fired twice in two days. REDS %327 was a Pond enclosure record disagreeing with the launcher it describes on the state-room side -- a per-host config pinning an elder the launcher had already adopted away. %335 is the same shape on the declaration side. The class is a record standing beside a launcher that nothing compares, and pond_policy_launcher is the loom for it rather than a third hand-check.
obs No meter could have fired, and the reason is worth keeping. pond_enclosure_policy read GREEN through all of this because it asks whether every declaration PLACES, never whether any declaration is TRUE. A guard that validates a document's grammar cannot see that the document describes the wrong world. That is %304's sentence one instrument over -- two spellings that must agree are one spelling plus a waiting bug -- and it is why the new guard reads the PAIR rather than either file.
obs The control refuses to be a one-sided proof. 25 behaviors on real planted trees: %335 itself planted and removed, the same closure row proven for gpu so the check is the roster rather than one word, an open claim standing with no flag behind it, a mount the record never declared refusing and then going green two ways -- by removing it from the launcher AND by declaring it in the record, which is what makes it a seam guard rather than a launcher guard -- both reported readings moving without gating, and both bounds shown one entry past and one entry under. Two cases failed on the first run because my seds anchored on --no-docker when the flag line ends --no-gpu; the control caught its own author.
obs What this does not reach, said plainly rather than left for a reader to discover. The record declares six mounts where ai-jail assembles about twenty-five: seven ro-binds, --dev /dev, --proc /proc, --dev-bind /dev/shm, and two rw-binds to the host's X11 socket directory and /run/user/1000 all stand undeclared, because they come from the jail's own defaults rather than from a flag the launcher passes. The new gate reads zero honestly today only because it compares what the launcher SPELLS. Declaring the jail's defaults is orbit two's own lap, and I have written it on the card rather than claimed it here.
obs The REDS pin is now the structural item. %335 cost 2,757 bytes and left construction/REDS.md at 24,519 of 24,576 -- 57 bytes. The next red genuinely has nowhere to go, so reds_fold.sh refusing OPEN rows moved from a tidy seat to a blocking one, and the card says so with the number rather than the adjective.
obs QA on touch, graded at molt time rather than queue time: the scan A+ 98, the card A 94, the record's comments A+ 98, all meter setting. No frame pushed. My first grading run read C+ because I handed --service 4 to a reading scaled 0 to 100 -- the tool was right and my input was wrong.
obs The council rota read row 2, Fire and Mars, cut and stop: N is git rev-list --count HEAD at 3652 and 3652 mod 5 is 2; the prior lap read row 4, so no repeat and no hand advance was owed. The row named this lap before I did. Its cardinal is the Standfast, which is what the four roster reds became; its fixed seat is reds-first and the allocation, which is where the hours went; and its dual is the three depths of removal, which is the discipline that kept me from rewriting anything ai-jail owns under the accrete-only boundary.
obs The send met the derived spine working exactly as written. I booked %329 from a fetched next_free of 329, and while I worked xy moved twelve commits: a peer had booked %329 at stamp 20260828.202405, two minutes and sixteen seconds before mine, and folded it onto construction/archive/REDS-the-mark-and-the-meter-rows-329.md where my grep of the living pin could not see it. Theirs is shared and frozen; mine was unshared, so mine derived above to %333 and every citation moved with it. Then it happened AGAIN on the next fetch: a peer published %333 at stamp 20260828.212447, later than mine, and a published number never moves however its stamp reads -- so mine derived once more, to %335. Two renumbers in one send, both mechanical, both because a number is a view allocated by the anointed remote while the stamp is the key.
obs The same rebase folded tools/fixtures/ into letter rooms, so my two new fixtures moved to tools/fixtures/p/ and took the peers' depth-proof root walk -- climb to the first ancestor holding rishi/bin and tools/fixtures, bounded at eight steps -- rather than the ../.. arithmetic that was correct one directory shallower. References are promises: four files carried the elder path and all four were repointed before the commit, with the whole-tree sweep run rather than a scoped one.
obs Writing the row short was forced and is worth recording as a measurement rather than a complaint. The ledger had 811 bytes free after the peers' fold and their three new rows; my first draft was 2,757 and the row that fits is 840. The three fields survive and the detail moved here, which is the division the card already asks for -- the card keeps the live edge, the logs keep the account. Both pins now sit within tens of bytes of their bounds: REDS at 24,569 of 24,576 and the card at 24,531 of 24,576.
obs The row did not land, and that is the round's second finding. `construction/REDS.md` stands at 24,571 of its 24,576 bound after the evening's fold, and my row is 804 bytes, so `tools/hooks/pre-commit` refused the commit outright -- not a guard reporting after the fact, a wall at write time. Every row in the pin reads OPEN, and the fold tool's normal use is closed rows: the peers' own fold this evening moved a row marked CLOSED. So booking a red now requires folding an OPEN row, which the card names as a seat. The row text is written and correct, parked rather than lost, and its whole content is above in this log. The card already carried the warning in a peer's words -- a full row still wants the seat -- and now carries the fact that it blocked one.
obs The gate arrived where the tree predicted it. An earlier card said in as many words that %327 and %328 were written short to fit and the next red has no room. I am the next red. That is a forecast landing rather than a surprise, and it means the seat is no longer about tidiness: an unbookable red breaks reds-first at the root, because the ledger is where a red becomes a lesson rather than a memory.
